y Li have helped shape strategies for improving mobile banking accessibility and appeal, making their contributions a critical resource for other researchers and practitioners [Data: Entities (213)].\n\n## Laukkanen's contributions to understanding barriers in mobile banking\n\nLaukkanen is renowned for their extensive work on the acceptance of mobile banking, particularly in identifying barriers to adoption. Their research provides a comprehensive understanding of the challenges users face, contributing significantly to the academic discourse on gender differences in mobile banking acceptance. Laukkanen's studies are pivotal in advancing knowledge on how demographic factors influence user behavior, offering practical implications for enhancing user engagement [Data: Entities (214)].\n\n## Collaborative efforts between Cruz and Laforet\n\nCruz and Laforet have collaborated on research focusing on gender differences in mobile banking acceptance. Their joint studies delve into how demographic factors, particularly gender, influence the adoption and usage of mobile banking services. This collaboration has provided valuable insights into behavioral patterns and preferences, helping to understand the barriers and motivators affecting mobile banking acceptance. Their work offers practical implications for financial institutions seeking to enhance user engagement across diverse customer segments [Data: Relationships (217)].\n\n## Li and Laukkanen's co-authored studies\n\nLi and Laukkanen have co-authored studies on gender differences in mobile banking acceptance. Their collaborative research has been crucial in identifying the factors that influence technology adoption, particularly among different genders. This partnership has advanced the discourse on mobile banking acceptance, providing a nuanced perspective that considers diverse user needs and behaviors [Data: Relationships (222)].\n\n## Laforet and Li's joint research contributions\n\nLaforet and Li have collaborated on research focusing on the acceptance of mobile banking, with an emphasis on gender differences. Their joint efforts have provided valuable insights into the factors influencing mobile banking technologies' acceptance, highlighting the role of gender in shaping user experiences and preferences. This collaboration has significantly advanced the understanding of mobile banking acceptance, offering a comprehensive analysis of user behavior across gender lines [Data: Relationships (220)].\n\n## Pasanen's role in mobile banking research\n\nPasanen, although less prominent than other researchers in this community, has conducted studies on the acceptance of mobile banking. Their work contributes to the broader understanding of how users perceive and adopt mobile banking technologies. Pasanen's research, in collaboration with Laukkanen, adds depth to the community's collective knowledge on mobile banking acceptance [Data: Entities (215); Relationships (224)].", "# PSD2 and Its Impact on European Payment Services\n\nThe community is centered around the Payment Services Directive 2 (PSD2), a significant regulatory framework in the European Union aimed at enhancing security and competition in the payment services sector. PSD2 has introduced new service providers like Third Party Providers (TPP) and Payment Initiation Services (PIS), fostering innovation and competition. It also mandates strong customer authentication measures and is linked to legal regulations such as the Penal Code for addressing crimes in mobile banking. The directive's implementation has significant implications for banks, service providers, and consumers across the EU.\n\n## PSD2's Role in Enhancing Security and Competition\n\nPSD2 is a pivotal directive in the European Union, designed to regulate payment services and providers. It was introduced to address technological advancements in the payment market, with a focus on enhancing security and fostering competition. The directive mandates strong customer authentication measures for online transactions, ensuring higher security standards. This is crucial in the digital age, where cyber threats are prevalent. By facilitating the emergence of new service providers like Third Party Providers (TPP) and Payment Initiation Services (PIS), PSD2 encourages innovation and competition within the financial sector [Data: Entities (360); Relationships (739, 740)].\n\n## Introduction of Third Party Providers (TPP) and Payment Initiation Services (PIS)\n\nPSD2 has introduced Third Party Providers (TPP) and Payment Initiation Services (PIS) as new players in the payment market. TPPs can initiate payments and access bank account data with customer consent, while PIS uses TPP to execute payments. This development has opened up the market to new entrants, challenging traditional banks and encouraging innovation. The presence of TPPs and PIS is expected to increase competition, leading to better services and lower costs for consumers [Data: Entities (471, 472); Relationships (739, 740)].\n\n## Account Information Service (AIS) and Its Benefits\n\nThe Account Information Service (AIS) is another significant feature enabled by PSD2. AIS allows access to bank accounts across different banks, facilitating credit assessments and account balance checks. This service provides consumers with a comprehensive view of their financial situation, enabling better financial management. For banks and financial institutions, AIS offers opportunities to develop new products and services tailored to consumer needs, thereby enhancing customer satisfaction and loyalty [Data: Entities (473); Relationships (741)].\n\n## Legal Compliance and the Role of Penal Code\n\nThe implementation of PSD2 is closely linked to legal regulations such as the Penal Code, which addresses crimes in mobile banking. This connection underscores the importance of legal compliance in the financial sector, particularly in the context of digital transactions. By aligning with legal frameworks, PSD2 aims to create a secure and trustworthy environment for consumers and service providers alike. This legal backing is essential for maintaining the integrity of the financial system and protecting against fraud and other criminal activities [Data: Entities (479); Relationships (742)].\n\n## Biometric Solutions as Part of PSD2's Security Measures\n\nBiometric solutions are part of the security measures introduced by PSD2, as discussed by sources like Kulas and Kowańska. These solutions enhance the security of online transactions by providing an additional layer of authentication. The use of biometrics, such as fingerprint or facial recognition, helps prevent unauthorized access and reduces the risk of fraud. This focus on security is a key aspect of PSD2, reflecting its commitment to protecting consumers in the digital payment landscape [Data: Entities (462, 463); Relationships (712, 713)].", "# Eurostat and the European Union\n\nThis community is centered around Eurostat, the statistical office of the European Union, which plays a pivotal role in providing statistical data to support the EU's decision-making processes. Eurostat's relationship with the European Union and its provision of data to member states like Poland highlights its importance in shaping EU policies and initiatives.\n\n## Eurostat's role as the EU's statistical office\n\nEurostat is the statistical office of the European Union, tasked with providing comprehensive statistical information to the EU institutions. It plays a crucial role in the analysis of population structures and other key statistical areas, supporting the EU's decision-making processes with reliable and relevant data. By offering a wide range of statistics, Eurostat enables informed policy-making and helps to ensure that the EU's strategies and initiatives are based on accurate and up-to-date information [Data: Entities (236)].\n\n## Eurostat's relationship with the European Union\n\nEurostat is intrinsically linked to the European Union, as it provides statistical information that is essential for the EU's functioning. This relationship underscores Eurostat's importance in the EU's political and economic union, as it supplies the data necessary for understanding and addressing various challenges faced by the member states [Data: Relationships (255)].\n\n## Impact of Eurostat's data on Poland\n\nEurostat has provided statistical data about the population distribution among different generations in Poland. This data is crucial for understanding demographic trends and planning for future needs in Poland, such as social services, healthcare, and economic policies. The provision of such data highlights Eurostat's role in supporting individual member states with tailored statistical insights [Data: Relationships (254)].\n\n## Eurostat's influence on EU policy-making\n\nThe data provided by Eurostat is instrumental in shaping EU policies and initiatives. By offering accurate and up-to-date statistics, Eurostat ensures that the EU's strategies are based on solid evidence, which is vital for effective governance and decision-making. This influence extends to various sectors, including economic, social, and environmental policies, demonstrating Eurostat's broad impact on the EU [Data: Entities (236)].\n\n## Eurostat's contribution to informed decision-making\n\nEurostat's comprehensive statistical information supports informed decision-making within the EU. By analyzing population structures and other key areas, Eurostat provides the data necessary for understanding complex issues and developing effective solutions. This contribution is essential for the EU's ability to address challenges and implement successful strategies across its member states [Data: Entities (236)].", "# Polish Financial Regulatory Framework: KNF and Key Banking Laws\n\nThis community is centered around the Polish financial regulatory framework, primarily involving the Komisja Nadzoru Finansowego (KNF), the Payment Services Act of 2011, and the Banking Law Act of 1997. The KNF plays a crucial role in overseeing the implementation of these laws to ensure financial market stability and security. The Payment Services Act has been amended to incorporate PSD2 regulations, highlighting the evolving nature of financial regulations in Poland.\n\n## Role of Komisja Nadzoru Finansowego (KNF)\n\nThe Komisja Nadzoru Finansowego (KNF) is the primary financial supervisory authority in Poland, tasked with overseeing the financial market, including the security of mobile banking. The KNF's responsibilities include ensuring the stability and security of the financial system, which is crucial for maintaining public confidence in the financial sector. The KNF's oversight extends to the implementation of key financial laws, such as the Banking Law Act of 1997, which regulates banking operations and ensures the security of stored funds [Data: Entities (468); Relationships (734)].\n\n## Significance of the Payment Services Act of 2011\n\nThe Payment Services Act of August 19, 2011, is a critical piece of legislation that outlines the responsibilities of payment service providers and users, with a focus on transaction security and risk management. This act has been amended to incorporate PSD2 regulations, reflecting the need to adapt to new technological advancements and security challenges in the financial sector. The incorporation of PSD2 highlights the act's role in enhancing the security and efficiency of payment services in Poland [Data: Entities (470); Relationships (738)].\n\n## Banking Law Act of 1997 and its Implementation\n\nThe Banking Law Act of August 29, 1997, is a fundamental legal framework that regulates banking operations in Poland. It ensures the security of stored funds and provides a legal basis for banking activities. The KNF oversees the implementation of this act to maintain financial market stability, which is essential for the smooth functioning of the banking sector. The act's provisions are designed to protect consumers and ensure the integrity of the banking system [Data: Entities (469); Relationships (734)].\n\n## Incorporation of PSD2 Regulations\n\nThe amendment of the Payment Services Act to incorporate PSD2 regulations signifies a major step in aligning Poland's financial regulations with European standards. PSD2 aims to enhance consumer protection, promote innovation, and improve the security of payment services. This incorporation reflects Poland's commitment to adopting international best practices in financial regulation, which is crucial for fostering trust and confidence in the financial system [Data: Relationships (738)].\n\n## Impact on Financial Market Stability\n\nThe combined efforts of the KNF and the implementation of key financial laws, such as the Payment Services Act and the Banking Law Act, play a vital role in ensuring financial market stability in Poland. These regulations are designed to mitigate risks, protect consumers, and maintain the integrity of the financial system. The stability of the financial market is essential for economic growth and development, making these entities and laws highly impactful [Data: Entities (468, 470, 469); Relationships (734, 738)].", "# Mobile Banking Usage in Polish Voivodeships\n\nThis community report focuses on the usage of mobile banking applications across various voivodeships in Poland. Key entities include regions such as Świętokrzyskie, Opolskie, and Dolnośląskie, each demonstrating varying levels of engagement with mobile banking technologies. The report highlights the disparities in mobile banking adoption, with Dolnośląskie leading in usage, while Świętokrzyskie and Opolskie show lower engagement. The analysis provides insights into regional trends and the potential impact on financial technology adoption in Poland.\n\n## Dolnośląskie as a leader in mobile banking usage\n\nDolnośląskie stands out as a leader in mobile banking application usage in Poland. The region's advanced development and progressive approach to financial technology are reflected in its residents' active engagement with mobile banking applications. This leadership position highlights Dolnośląskie's role in setting trends for digital banking within the country, showcasing how technology is integrated into everyday financial practices. The high adoption rate in Dolnośląskie serves as a benchmark for other regions aiming to enhance their digital banking infrastructure [Data: Entities (338); Relationships (503)].\n\n## Lower mobile banking usage in Świętokrzyskie and Opolskie\n\nBoth Świętokrzyskie and Opolskie are noted for their lower than average rates of mobile banking application usage compared to other regions in Poland. Despite being recognized in discussions related to mobile banking, these regions have not embraced the technology as extensively as others. This suggests potential barriers to adoption, such as lack of awareness, technological infrastructure, or demographic factors that may influence the utilization of mobile banking services. Understanding these barriers is crucial for financial institutions aiming to increase adoption rates in these areas [Data: Entities (333, 335); Relationships (497, 499)].\n\n## Significance of mobile banking applications\n\nMobile banking applications are pivotal in modern financial systems, offering convenience and security for users to perform banking transactions from their mobile devices. These applications are equipped with various security measures, including transaction limits, to ensure the safety of users' financial data. The analysis of mobile banking usage patterns across different demographics and regions in Poland provides valuable insights for banks and financial institutions. By understanding how factors such as age, income level, and geographic location influence usage, these institutions can tailor their services to meet diverse customer needs and enhance user experience [Data: Entities (342); Relationships (503, 507, 510, 512, 513, 519, 520)].\n\n## Regional disparities in mobile banking adoption\n\nThe report highlights significant regional disparities in mobile banking adoption across Poland. While regions like Dolnośląskie and Warszawski stołeczny exhibit high usage rates, others like Świętokrzyskie and Opolskie lag behind. These disparities may be attributed to differences in technological infrastructure, economic development, and demographic factors. Addressing these disparities is essential for ensuring equitable access to digital financial services and fostering a more inclusive financial ecosystem in Poland [Data: Entities (333, 335, 338, 341); Relationships (497, 499, 503, 506)].\n\n## Potential for growth in mobile banking usage\n\nDespite the current disparities, there is significant potential for growth in mobile banking usage across Poland. As financial institutions continue to innovate and improve their digital offerings, regions with lower adoption rates may see increased engagement. Efforts to enhance technological infrastructure, increase awareness, and address demographic barriers can contribute to higher adoption rates. This growth potential presents opportunities for financial institutions to expand their customer base and improve financial inclusion in underserved regions [Data: Entities (333, 335, 338); Relationships (497, 499, 503)].", "# Mobile Banking in Poland: Millennium Bank and Regional Usage\n\nThe community centers around mobile banking applications in Poland, with Millennium Bank as a key player. The report highlights the varying adoption rates of mobile banking across different Polish regions, with Eastern Poland showing lower usage compared to Western Poland. The integration of services like BLIK, moBiLET, and Google Assistant into mobile banking applications reflects the technological advancements in the sector.\n\n## Millennium Bank's pivotal role in mobile banking\n\nMillennium Bank is a prominent financial institution in Poland, recognized for its comprehensive range of mobile banking services. The bank's commitment to integrating advanced technology into its offerings is exemplified by its popular BLIK services, a mobile payment system that allows users to make transactions quickly and securely using their smartphones. This focus on mobile banking highlights Millennium Bank's dedication to meeting the evolving needs of its customers by providing accessible and efficient financial services [Data: Entities (362); Relationships (551)].\n\n## Regional disparities in mobile banking usage\n\nThere is a notable disparity in mobile banking application usage across different regions in Poland. Eastern Poland, including voivodeships like Podlaskie, Lubelskie, and Podkarpackie, exhibits a lower than average rate of mobile banking application usage. This suggests that while mobile banking is present in these regions, its adoption and utilization by the population are not as widespread as in other parts of the country. In contrast, Western Poland generally shows higher usage rates, indicating a more robust adoption of digital financial services [Data: Entities (329, 331, 332, 336, 337); Relationships (493, 495, 496, 500, 501)].\n\n## Technological integration in mobile banking\n\nMobile banking applications in Poland have integrated various technological services to enhance user experience. For instance, moBiLET is a service for purchasing public transportation tickets through mobile banking applications, and Google Assistant is used for voice-activated tasks. These integrations reflect the rapid technological advancements and the shift towards more digital and user-friendly banking solutions in Poland. Such innovations are indicative of the broader trend towards digitalization in the financial sector [Data: Entities (363, 364); Relationships (546, 547)].\n\n## Blue Media's contribution to mobile banking\n\nBlue Media, a company offering automatic toll payments on highways through the Autopay service, is another significant player in the mobile banking landscape. The Autopay service is accessible through mobile banking applications, providing users with a convenient and efficient way to manage toll payments. This service exemplifies the integration of practical solutions into mobile banking platforms, enhancing the overall utility and appeal of these applications for consumers [Data: Entities (361, 365); Relationships (537, 538)].\n\n## Impact of mobile banking on consumer behavior\n\nThe introduction and adoption of mobile banking applications in Poland have significantly transformed consumer behavior in the banking sector. From 2011 to 2013, there was a widespread offering of mobile banking applications by various banks across the country. This period marked a major development in the Polish banking industry, as these applications became increasingly popular among consumers. The study on consumer behavior aimed to understand how Polish consumers interacted with these mobile banking applications, providing insights into their preferences, usage patterns, and the overall impact on their banking experience [Data: Relationships (27)].", "# Mobile Banking and Generational Dynamics in Poland\n\nThis community report focuses on the intersection of mobile banking technology and generational demographics in Poland, highlighting key regions and demographic cohorts such as Baby Boomers, Generation A, and the Silent Generation. The report examines the adoption of mobile banking across different generations, with a particular focus on the regions of Wielkopolskie and Śląskie, which are noted for their advanced technological engagement. The influence of social policies like Program 500+ on demographic trends is also considered.\n\n## Baby Boomers' cautious approach to mobile banking\n\nBaby Boomers in Poland, born between 1946 and 1964, represent a significant demographic cohort that has been the subject of studies focusing on their acceptance and use of mobile banking. This generation places a higher emphasis on security and trustworthiness compared to younger generations, which prioritize convenience and speed. Financial institutions must address these trust factors by ensuring robust security measures and clear communication about safety protocols to engage Baby Boomers effectively. Understanding these specific needs is crucial for banks to enhance user satisfaction and encourage greater adoption of mobile banking among this demographic [Data: Entities (195); Relationships (197, 231)].\n\n## Wielkopolskie and Śląskie as leaders in mobile banking adoption\n\nThe regions of Wielkopolskie and Śląskie in Poland are recognized for their advanced development and active engagement in mobile banking. Residents of these regions frequently use mobile banking applications, reflecting their integration of digital solutions into everyday life. This highlights the regions' commitment to embracing technological advancements and improving the convenience and efficiency of financial transactions. As well-developed areas, Wielkopolskie and Śląskie stand out in Poland for their progressive approach to technology and finance, serving as notable examples of how regions can leverage digital tools to enhance the quality of life for their residents [Data: Entities (346, 347); Relationships (514, 518)].\n\n## Impact of Program 500+ on Generation A\n\nProgram 500+ is a social policy initiative in Poland aimed at increasing birth rates by providing financial support to families. This program has been credited with influencing the birth rate of Generation A, a demographic cohort comprising individuals born after 2010. As digital natives, members of Generation A are expected to be highly adept at using technology, having been exposed to it from a very young age. This exposure is likely to influence their learning, communication, and interaction with the world, setting them apart from previous generations. The program's impact on demographic trends underscores its significance in shaping the future technological landscape in Poland [Data: Entities (238, 241); Relationships (268)].\n\n## The Silent Generation's traditional values and resilience\n\nThe Silent Generation, consisting of individuals born between 1928 and 1945, represents the oldest generation in Poland. This group is characterized by their experiences and historical context, having lived through significant global events such as the Great Depression, World War II, and the post-war recovery period. Known for their traditional values, strong work ethic, and resilience, the Silent Generation's influence on societal trends and economic patterns is notable. Despite their smaller population size compared to other cohorts, their historical experiences continue to shape modern societal values and economic behaviors in Poland [Data: Entities (237); Relationships (264)].\n\n## Poland's role in the evolution of mobile banking technologies\n\nPoland has been a focal point for studies on the use of mobile applications, particularly in the banking sector. Research conducted in the country has highlighted the usage of mobile banking applications, with a specific focus on the north-eastern and south-eastern regions. Historically, Poland has seen a popularity in mobile banking and the use of devices such as palmtops, indicating a trend towards technological adoption in financial services. This context underscores Poland's role as a significant player in the evolution of mobile banking technologies, influencing both regional and global trends in digital finance [Data: Entities (30); Relationships (326)].", "# University of Economics in Poznań and Technology Acceptance Model\n\nThis community is centered around the University of Economics in Poznań, specifically its Department of Market and Service Research, and the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM). The university is involved in quantitative research on consumer payment and purchasing behaviors, while TAM is a framework used to study technology adoption. The relationship between these entities highlights a focus on understanding and improving technology acceptance in economic contexts.\n\n## Role of the University of Economics in Poznań\n\nThe University of Economics in Poznań is a key entity in this community, playing a crucial role in conducting research on consumer payment and purchasing behaviors. This research is essential for understanding market trends and consumer preferences, which can significantly impact economic strategies and policies. The university's involvement in such research underscores its importance in the academic and economic landscape [Data: Entities (267)].\n\n## Significance of the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM)\n\nThe Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) is a pivotal framework within this community, used to study the adoption of technology. Its application in research conducted by entities like the University of Economics in Poznań highlights its relevance in understanding how consumers accept and use new technologies. This understanding is critical for developing strategies to enhance technology adoption, which can drive innovation and economic growth [Data: Entities (175); Relationships (230)].\n\n## Integration of Market and Service Research\n\nThe Department of Market and Service Research at the University of Economics in Poznań is integral to the community's research efforts. As part of the university, this department focuses on quantitative research that informs both academic and practical applications in market analysis. Its work supports the university's mission to contribute to economic knowledge and policy-making [Data: Entities (268); Relationships (322)].\n\n## Collaboration between TAM and Oliveira\n\nThe collaboration between TAM and Oliveira in studies on mobile banking acceptance illustrates the interdisciplinary nature of research within this community. Such collaborations enhance the depth and breadth of research findings, providing comprehensive insights into technology adoption patterns. This partnership is indicative of the community's commitment to advancing knowledge in technology acceptance [Data: Relationships (230)].\n\n## Impact on Economic and Technological Development\n\nThe research conducted by the University of Economics in Poznań and its use of the Technology Acceptance Model have significant implications for economic and technological development. By understanding consumer behavior and technology adoption, the community can influence policy-making and strategic planning, fostering an environment conducive to innovation and growth. This impact underscores the community's importance in shaping future economic landscapes [Data: Entities (267, 175)].", "# Mazowieckie and Warsaw Mobile Banking Community\n\nThe community is centered around the Mazowieckie voivodeship in Poland, with a particular focus on Warsaw, the capital city. This region is notable for its advanced technological development, especially in the realm of mobile banking. The high adoption rate of mobile banking applications among residents of Mazowieckie, and particularly in Warsaw, underscores the community's inclination towards modern financial solutions.\n\n## Mazowieckie's Technological Advancement\n\nMazowieckie is recognized for its advanced development, particularly in the field of mobile banking. The region's technological infrastructure supports a high rate of mobile banking usage, indicating a strong inclination towards modern financial solutions. This technological advancement positions Mazowieckie as a leader in financial technology within Poland, potentially influencing other regions to adopt similar innovations [Data: Entities (327)].\n\n## Warsaw's Role in Mobile Banking Adoption\n\nWarsaw, as the capital city located within the Mazowieckie voivodeship, plays a crucial role in the adoption of mobile banking. The city's residents exhibit a high percentage of mobile banking users, reflecting the broader trend within the voivodeship. Warsaw's position as a major urban center contributes to its residents' readiness to embrace technological advancements, further driving the region's reputation as a hub for financial innovation [Data: Entities (328); Relationships (491)].\n\n## Economic Implications of Mobile Banking\n\nThe widespread adoption of mobile banking in Mazowieckie has significant economic implications. It facilitates easier access to financial services, promotes financial inclusion, and supports the growth of digital economies. The region's focus on mobile banking can attract investments in fintech and related sectors, potentially boosting economic growth and creating new job opportunities [Data: Entities (327)].\n\n## Potential for Regional Influence\n\nMazowieckie's success in mobile banking adoption positions it as a potential influencer for other regions in Poland and beyond. The voivodeship's approach to integrating technology into everyday financial transactions can serve as a model for other areas looking to enhance their technological capabilities. This influence can extend to policy-making, encouraging the development of supportive regulations for fintech innovations [Data: Entities (327)].\n\n## Challenges and Risks\n\nDespite the benefits, the high reliance on mobile banking in Mazowieckie also presents challenges and risks. These include cybersecurity threats, data privacy concerns, and the potential for digital exclusion among populations less familiar with technology. Addressing these challenges is crucial to ensuring the sustainable growth of mobile banking in the region and maintaining public trust in digital financial services [Data: Entities (327)].", "# Mobile Banking Ecosystem: Key Entities and Innovations\n\nThe community is centered around the concept of mobile banking, with banks as the primary entities offering services through various digital channels. Key contributors to the understanding and definition of mobile banking include researchers such as Shaikh, Karjaluoto, Thakur, and others. The ecosystem is supported by technological advancements like smartphones, digital wallets, and smartwatches, which facilitate mobile banking services. The OECD's Oslo methodology also plays a role in guiding innovations within this sector.\n\n## Banks as Central Entities in Mobile Banking\n\nBanks are the central entities in the mobile banking ecosystem, offering a range of services through mobile applications. They operate within the mobile banking market, leveraging technological advancements to enhance customer interaction and service delivery. The integration of mobile phones and smartphones has been pivotal in enabling banks to offer these services, making them accessible to a broader audience [Data: Entities (21); Relationships (39, 41, 42)].\n\n## Role of Researchers in Defining Mobile Banking\n\nSeveral researchers have significantly contributed to the understanding and definition of mobile banking. Notable figures include Shaikh, Karjaluoto, Thakur, Barnes, S. J., Corbitt, B., Suoranta, M., Mattila, M., and Świecka, B. Their work has helped shape the conceptual framework of mobile banking as a channel for customer interaction and a method of conducting financial services using mobile communication techniques [Data: Entities (22, 23, 24, 25, 26, 27, 28, 29); Relationships (31, 32, 33, 34, 35, 36, 37, 38)].\n\n## Technological Advancements Supporting Mobile Banking\n\nThe evolution of mobile banking has been significantly influenced by technological advancements such as smartphones, digital wallets, and smartwatches. Smartphones are the primary devices used for mobile banking transactions, while digital wallets and smartwatches offer additional convenience and functionality for users. These technologies have expanded the capabilities of mobile banking, making it more versatile and user-friendly [Data: Entities (32, 34, 35); Relationships (42, 44, 45)].\n\n## OECD's Influence on Banking Innovations\n